From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:30:10 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Leave the PMC power state to
 the boot loader

With the PMP handed to Linux, its firmware crashes right after the
driver starts it:

  apple_pmp 380500000.pmp: RTKit: co-processor has crashed
    ESR = 0xbe000000 (SError), FAR = 0x382800004

0x382800000 is the PMC, the SoC's performance-state voter block (the
"voters" window of apple,t8132-pmc). In the J604 device tree the PMP is
voter agent 0 (/arm-io/pmgr voter-agent, pmp-voter-agent-idx), so the
fault is the PMP firmware's own first vote. The PMC is gated by the PMGR
device "PMC" (id 0x14), whose power state the J604 device tree places at
PMGR 0x380700000 + 0x138, which is ps_pmc.

The boot loader leaves that power state on: genpd's accounting for "pmc"
shows it registered on and switched off about 140 ms later. Linux has no
consumer for it, so genpd gates it as unused at the end of kernel init
("PM: genpd: Disabling unused power domains"), before the PMP module is
loaded from the root file system. When apple_pmp then starts the
firmware, its access to the unpowered PMC is answered with an SError.

The J713 never registers this power state (its PMGR policy disables
ps_pmc) and hands the PMP over with the PMC as the boot loader left it.
Do the same on the J604. The PMGR device entry for the PMC is the same in
the 25G83 and 26B123 j604ap device trees as in the j713ap ones.

The domains the PMP tracks for power (its soc-device list: DISP,
DISPEXT0/1, ANE0, ATC0-3 and so on) are handled by their own drivers, as
on macOS. The PMC is not one of them.

Fixes: ac4e9ecec652 ("arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: Hand the PMP to Linux")
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
index ce2afdbc25..83843651a3 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
@@ -619,6 +619,19 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * The PMC (the SoC's performance-state voter block at 0x382800000) is the
+ * PMP firmware's: the PMP votes in it as voter agent 0 from its first
+ * steps. The boot loader leaves it powered and Linux has no consumer for
+ * it, so registering its power state would only let genpd gate it as unused
+ * before the PMP driver loads, and the PMP firmware then crashes on its
+ * first vote with an SError. Leave it as the boot loader left it, as the
+ * J713 does.
+ */
+&ps_pmc {
+	status = "disabled";
+};
+
 &aop {
 	status = "okay"; /* DTV-AUD-1, DTV-AUD-2, DTV-AUD-13 */
 };
